@startuml CloudWarehouse_ADR_Decisions
title Architecture Decisions (ADR) - Top
top to bottom direction
skinparam defaultFontSize 10
skinparam rectangleBorderColor #34495e
skinparam packageStyle rectangle

package "Architecture Decisions (ADR)" #E8F4FC {

  together {
    rectangle "ADR-1\nDapper (not EF Core)" as ADR1 #E8F4FC
    rectangle "ADR-2\nMonolithic Web API" as ADR2 #E8F4FC
    rectangle "ADR-3\nRules via Excel only" as ADR3 #E8F4FC
  }
  together {
    rectangle "ADR-4\nAll-or-nothing import" as ADR4 #E8F4FC
    rectangle "ADR-5\nClosedXML + dual headers" as ADR5 #E8F4FC
    rectangle "ADR-6\nGitHub Actions CI" as ADR6 #E8F4FC
  }

  ADR1 -[hidden]right- ADR2
  ADR2 -[hidden]right- ADR3
  ADR4 -[hidden]right- ADR5
  ADR5 -[hidden]right- ADR6
  ADR1 -[hidden]down- ADR4
}

note bottom of ADR1 : SQL control\nmanual schema.sql
note bottom of ADR2 : fast MVP\nsimple ops
note bottom of ADR3 : supplier spreadsheets\nno rule CRUD API
note bottom of ADR4 : SQL transaction\none error = rollback all
note bottom of ADR5 : .xlsx standard + legacy\nno .xls
note bottom of ADR6 : CI evidence video\nno Jenkins

@enduml

' 1. Six ADRs cover data access, deployment shape, rule source, import consistency, Excel, and CI.
' 2. Each decision trades flexibility for speed and clarity within course scope.
' 3. The most business-critical ADR is all-or-nothing import with full batch rollback.
